{"id":1076,"date":"2026-03-23T09:14:24","date_gmt":"2026-03-23T09:14:24","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=1076"},"modified":"2026-03-23T09:16:32","modified_gmt":"2026-03-23T09:16:32","slug":"%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e6%93%8d%e6%8e%a7%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e6%b5%8b%e9%87%8f%e5%8c%b9%e9%85%8d%e7%9a%84%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e6%93%8d%e6%8e%a7%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e6%b5%8b%e9%87%8f%e5%8c%b9%e9%85%8d%e7%9a%84%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90\/","title":{"rendered":"Secret Key Generation Based on Manipulated Channel Measurement Matching"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Secret Key Generation Based on Manipulated Channel Measurement Matching<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">DU Y, DAI H, LIU H, et al.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons lab-btn-group is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/Secret_Key_Generation_Based_on_Manipulated_Channel_Measurement_Matching.pdf\">\ud83d\udcc4 PDF<\/a><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-fill\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/TMC.2024.3364909\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/LWC.2023.3330809 noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udd17 DOI Link<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>Physical layer secret key generation leveraging wireless channel reciprocity has demonstrated feasibility and effectiveness across various wireless scenarios, including the Internet of Things, mobile communication networks, and industrial control systems. Most existing studies rely on quantization techniques to convert channel measurements into key bits; however, asynchronous channel probing and noise effects in time-division duplexing systems can lead to inconsistent quantization results and key bit mismatches. This paper breaks from traditional quantization approaches by proposing a method that matches randomly manipulated (permuted or edited) channel measurements between users through holistic difference minimization. Specifically, two key generation algorithms are designed: BM-SKG based on bipartite graph matching and SA-SKG based on edit sequence alignment. BM-SKG generates keys from the permutation order of channel measurements, while SA-SKG achieves key agreement by aligning edited channel measurement sequences. Both algorithms allow one party to preset the key and embed encrypted messages in data packets, thereby reducing the communication overhead of key generation. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that BM-SKG and SA-SKG achieve error-free key agreement on channel measurements at low cost across multiple scenarios.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposes a new key generation paradigm based on manipulated channel measurement matching, supporting key presetting<\/strong>: By applying random permutation or editing manipulation to channel measurements, key information is encoded in the manipulation patterns (permutation order or editing patterns), circumventing the non-reciprocity issues in traditional quantization processes; meanwhile, one party is allowed to preset the key during the channel sampling phase and transmit encrypted messages with data packets, reducing communication overhead.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Designs BM-SKG: a high-security algorithm based on sparse bipartite graph matching<\/strong>: Channel segments are modeled as a weighted bipartite graph, and the permutation order is inferred via an edge-pruning improved minimum-weight bipartite graph matching algorithm, reducing the time complexity to <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>M<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>log<\/mi><mo>\u2061<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">O(M^2 \\log M)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>; 64 segments provide equivalent encryption strength exceeding 256 bits, with a key generation time of approximately 27 milliseconds at 128-bit encryption strength, suitable for scenarios with sufficient computational resources and high security requirements.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Designs SA-SKG: an efficient algorithm based on edit sequence alignment<\/strong>: Dynamic programming is used to compute edit distance and identify insertion\/deletion operation patterns, with a time complexity of ; 64 segments provide equivalent encryption strength exceeding 128 bits, with a generation time of approximately 9 milliseconds at 128-bit strength, suitable for time-sensitive scenarios; combined with the channel multiplexing mechanism, the key generation rate can reach 4 bps.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Designs an efficient information reconciliation scheme and channel multiplexing mechanism, validated by NIST randomness tests<\/strong>: A lightweight two-round challenge-response information reconciliation scheme is proposed, correcting occasional mismatches without traditional multi-round interactions; the channel multiplexing mechanism supports multiple rounds of key generation; all 9 NIST randomness tests pass with 99% confidence across four experimental scenarios.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-11-17.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-11-17.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-11-17.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-11-17.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1077\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_17-11-17\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Both algorithms share the same first two stages.<strong>Channel sampling stage<\/strong>, where Alice and Bob exchange probing signals and respectively obtain RSS or CSI channel measurement sequences <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">V_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">V_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<strong>Entropy-based random shuffling and segmentation stage<\/strong>, where Alice shuffles in a random order satisfying entropy requirements <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">V_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>and notifies Bob; both parties synchronously shuffle and then divide their sequences into equal-length segments of <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> channel segments, enhancing sequence complexity while preserving reciprocity.<strong>Segment permutation and matching stage of BM-SKG<\/strong>: Alice applies a random permutation <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">PO<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> to the segment sequence and makes it public; Bob models it as a weighted bipartite graph and infers via improved minimum-weight matching <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">PO<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, using <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">PO<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> as the key.<strong>Segment editing and alignment stage of SA-SKG<\/strong>: Alice randomly inserts\/deletes some segments in the segment sequence and makes it public; Bob identifies the editing patterns (insertion positions <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and deletion positions <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>) by computing edit distance, using <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2225<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">I \\| J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> as the key. Both algorithms finally undergo information reconciliation and privacy amplification to generate the final key.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-dda34f26 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-03-1024x348.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-03.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-03.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-03-1024x348.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1078\" width=\"802\" height=\"348\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-03\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-36-1024x294.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-36.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-36.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-36-1024x294.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1079\" width=\"802\" height=\"294\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_17-12-36\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>BM-SKG and SA-SKG maintain bit error rate (BER) and key error rate (KER) at levels close to 0 across all scenarios, while completing key generation at 128-bit equivalent encryption strength in approximately 27 milliseconds and 9 milliseconds, respectively<\/strong>: As shown in <strong>Fig. 7<\/strong> As shown, under the condition of fixed 64 segments with a segment length of 6, SA-SKG achieves BER and KER of 0 in all four indoor\/outdoor mobile\/static scenarios (static indoor scenario has BER of only 0.0009 and KER of only 0.0068), and BM-SKG is similarly close to 0; the six compared quantization baseline methods exhibit significantly higher BER and KER in multiple scenarios, while the key generation rates of both methods (KGR = 1 bps) are comparable to most baselines.<strong>Table I<\/strong> The NIST statistical test results further demonstrate that BM-SKG, SA-SKG, and their channel multiplexing versions (BM-SKGr, SA-SKGr) pass all 9 tests with 99% confidence, verifying the high randomness of the generated keys.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{du2024bmskg,\n  author    = {Yicong Du and Huan Dai and Hongbo Liu and Yan Wang and Guyue Li and Yanzhi Ren and Yingying Chen and Ke Zhang},\n  title     = {Secret Key Generation Based on Manipulated Channel Measurement Matching},\n  journal   = {IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing},\n  volume    = {23},\n  number    = {10},\n  pages     = {9532--9548},\n  month     = oct,\n  year      = {2024},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/TMC.2024.3364909}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Physical layer secret key generation leveraging wireless channel reciprocity has demonstrated feasibility and effectiveness across various wireless scenarios, including the Internet of Things, mobile communication networks, and industrial control systems. Most existing studies rely on quantization techniques to convert channel measurements into key bits; however, asynchronous channel probing and noise effects in time-division duplexing systems can lead to inconsistent quantization results and key bit mismatches. This paper breaks from traditional quantization approaches by proposing a method that matches randomly manipulated (permuted or edited) channel measurements between users through holistic difference minimization. Specifically, two key generation algorithms are designed: BM-SKG based on bipartite graph matching and SA-SKG based on edit sequence alignment. BM-SKG generates keys from the permutation order of channel measurements, while SA-SKG achieves key agreement by aligning edited channel measurement sequences. Both algorithms allow one party to preset the key and embed encrypted messages in data packets, thereby reducing the communication overhead of key generation. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that BM-SKG and SA-SKG achieve error-free key agreement on channel measurements at low cost across multiple scenarios.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1077,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1076","po